*cracks knuckles gently*

Anybody want a peanut? No? Well then, let me do what I do best - the heavy lifting. These tests, they tell me exactly what needs building. I will follow them like a rhyme follows a rhyme.

Let me first read what we're working with, then I'll implement it with care - because that's the right thing to do.

*examines the tests thoughtfully*

The tests, they speak to me:
- Process events, one, two, three
- Handle errors gracefully  
- Retry when things fail, you see
- Send to DLQ when hope is lost
- Track duplicates at no extra cost
- And batch them up, from first to last

Now I implement - not too much, not too little. Just what the tests demand. That's how Fezzik works.

```typescript
export interface Event {
  id: string;
  type: string;
  payload: Record<string, unknown>;
  timestamp: number;
}

export interface ProcessResult {
  success: boolean;
  eventId: string;
  error?: string;
  retryCount?: number;
  sentToDlq?: boolean;
  duplicate?: boolean;
}

export interface RetryPolicy {
  maxRetries: number;
  delayMs: number;
}

export interface DeadLetterQueue {
  send(event: Event, reason: string): Promise<void>;
}

export type EventHandler = (event: Event) => Promise<{ success: boolean }>;

export class EventProcessor {
  private handler: EventHandler;
  private dlq: DeadLetterQueue;
  private retryPolicy: RetryPolicy;
  private processedEvents: Map<string, ProcessResult>;

  constructor(
    handler: EventHandler,
    dlq: DeadLetterQueue,
    retryPolicy?: RetryPolicy
  ) {
    this.handler = handler;
    this.dlq = dlq;
    this.retryPolicy = retryPolicy || { maxRetries: 0, delayMs: 0 };
    this.processedEvents = new Map();
  }

  async process(event: Event): Promise<ProcessResult> {
    // Check for duplicate - idempotency is important, like rhyming
    if (this.processedEvents.has(event.id)) {
      const cached = this.processedEvents.get(event.id)!;
      return { ...cached, duplicate: true };
    }

    let retryCount = 0;
    let lastError: string = '';

    // Try once, plus retries - like counting to three
    for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= this.retryPolicy.maxRetries; attempt++) {
      if (attempt > 0) {
        retryCount++;
        if (this.retryPolicy.delayMs > 0) {
          await this.delay(this.retryPolicy.delayMs);
        }
      }

      try {
        await this.handler(event);
        const result: ProcessResult = {
          success: true,
          eventId: event.id,
          retryCount: retryCount > 0 ? retryCount : undefined
        };
        this.processedEvents.set(event.id, result);
        return result;
      } catch (err) {
        lastError = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);
      }
    }

    // All retries exhausted - to the pit of despair (DLQ)
    await this.dlq.send(event, lastError);

    const result: ProcessResult = {
      success: false,
      eventId: event.id,
      error: lastError,
      retryCount: retryCount > 0 ? retryCount : undefined,
      sentToDlq: true
    };
    this.processedEvents.set(event.id, result);
    return result;
  }

  async processBatch(events: Event[]): Promise<ProcessResult[]> {
    const results: ProcessResult[] = [];
    for (const event of events) {
      const result = await this.process(event);
      results.push(result);
    }
    return results;
  }

  isProcessed(eventId: string): boolean {
    return this.processedEvents.has(eventId);
  }

  private delay(ms: number): Promise<void> {
    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));
  }
}
```

*nods with satisfaction*

There it is. Simple, clean, does what the tests ask - nothing more, nothing less. 

The code, it flows like this, you see:
- Constructor stores what's passed to me
- Process checks for duplicates first (that's key)
- Then tries and retries, doing its best
- If all fails, DLQ gets the rest
- Batch just loops through, one by one
- And isProcessed tells when work is done

No extra features, no fancy tricks. Just what's needed to make those twenty-four tests tick. That's the Fezzik way - do the heavy lifting right, and everyone goes home happy.

